/**
 * @page xhal_concepts XHAL Concepts
 * @brief ChibiOS/XHAL Concepts
 * This page describes the driver model shared by all the XHAL drivers: the
 * classes, the driver states, the start and stop of drivers, the
 * configurations, the callbacks, the synchronous and asynchronous
 * operations and the structure of the platforms.
 * @note    XHAL is under development, this page describes the current
 *          implementation.
 *
 * - @ref xhal_overview
 * - @ref xhal_classes
 * - @ref xhal_states
 * - @ref xhal_start_stop
 * - @ref xhal_configurations
 * - @ref xhal_callbacks
 * - @ref xhal_operations
 * - @ref xhal_sharing
 * - @ref xhal_platforms
 * - @ref xhal_config
 * .
 *
 * @section xhal_overview Overview
 * XHAL is the object-oriented evolution of the ChibiOS HAL:
 * - Drivers are classes built on the common OOP base classes, all the
 *   stateful drivers derive from the same base driver class and share the
 *   same states and the same start, stop and configuration functions.
 * - The portable part of each driver is generated from an XML description,
 *   the hardware-dependent part is the low level driver (LLD) of the
 *   platform.
 * - XHAL uses the ChibiOS/RT or ChibiOS/NIL API directly, there is no OS
 *   abstraction layer.
 * - The API is similar to the classic HAL but not compatible with it. Most
 *   functions return an error code.
 * .
 * The application calls halInit() before chSysInit(). halInit() initializes
 * the platform, then each driver enabled in @p xhalconf.h, which initializes
 * the driver objects of the platform, then the board with boardInit() and
 * finally the system timer.<br>
 * The functions follow the naming and calling conventions of the kernel,
 * see @ref api_suffixes.
 *
 * @section xhal_classes Classes and Interfaces
 * The driver classes derive from two common classes:
 * - @p hal_base_driver_c, the ancestor of all the stateful drivers. It holds
 *   the driver state, the current configuration, a driver argument and,
 *   when enabled, a mutex and the registry data.
 * - @p hal_cb_driver_c, derived from @p hal_base_driver_c, it adds a
 *   callback, see @ref xhal_callbacks.
 * .
 * | Base class                 | Drivers                                    |
 * |----------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
 * | @p hal_cb_driver_c         | ADC, CAN, DAC, ETH, GPT, I2C, I2S, ICU, PWM, RTC, SDC, SIO, SPI, WSPI. |
 * | @p hal_base_driver_c       | Crypto, TRNG, USB, WDG.                    |
 * | @p hal_flash_base_c        | EFL and the serial NOR flash devices.      |
 *
 * PAL and the system timer (ST) are not classes.<br>
 * Drivers expose common interfaces, so code written for an interface works
 * with any driver implementing it:
 * | Interface                  | Implemented by                             |
 * |----------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
 * | @p asynchronous_channel_i  | SIO, buffered SIO, serial over USB.        |
 * | @p block_io_i              | SDC.                                       |
 * | @p flash_interface_i       | EFL, serial NOR flash devices.             |
 * | @p tty_i                   | POSIX TTY over SIO.                        |
 *
 * An interface is obtained from an object with @p oopGetIf(), for example
 * <tt>oopGetIf(&SIOD1, chn)</tt> returns the channel interface of a SIO
 * driver, usable by any code accepting a sequential stream.<br>
 * The driver objects are defined by the platform, for example @p SIOD1,
 * and exist when enabled in the MCU configuration, see
 * @ref xhal_platforms. The complex drivers in @p os/xhal/lib/complex are
 * built on other drivers, for example serial over USB, MMC over SPI, the
 * managed flash storage and the sensors. A complex driver owns the drivers
 * it uses: the buffered SIO, for example, starts and stops its SIO driver
 * and installs its own callback on it.
 *
 * @section xhal_states Driver States
 * All the stateful drivers have the same states, see drvGetStateX():
 *
 * @if LATEX_PDF
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    size="5, 7";
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=circle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.9", height="0.9"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    uninit [label="UNINIT", style="bold"];
    stop [label="STOP", shape=doublecircle];
    starting [label="STARTING"];
    ready [label="READY"];
    active [label="ACTIVE"];
    stopping [label="STOPPING"];
    uninit -> stop [label="xxxObjectInit()"];
    stop -> starting [label="drvStart()"];
    starting -> ready [label="started"];
    starting -> stop [label="start failed"];
    ready -> active [label="operation\nstarted"];
    active -> ready [label="operation\nended"];
    ready -> stopping [label="drvStop()"];
    active -> stopping [label="drvStop()"];
    stopping -> stop [label="stopped"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @else
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=circle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8, fixedsize="true", width="0.9", height="0.9"];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    uninit [label="UNINIT", style="bold"];
    stop [label="STOP", shape=doublecircle];
    starting [label="STARTING"];
    ready [label="READY"];
    active [label="ACTIVE"];
    stopping [label="STOPPING"];
    uninit -> stop [label="xxxObjectInit()"];
    stop -> starting [label="drvStart()"];
    starting -> ready [label="started"];
    starting -> stop [label="start failed"];
    ready -> active [label="operation\nstarted"];
    active -> ready [label="operation\nended"];
    ready -> stopping [label="drvStop()"];
    active -> stopping [label="drvStop()"];
    stopping -> stop [label="stopped"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @endif
 *
 * - <b>UNINIT</b>, the object is not initialized.
 * - <b>STOP</b>, the object is initialized and the peripheral is stopped.
 * - <b>STARTING</b>, the low level driver is starting the peripheral.
 * - <b>READY</b>, the peripheral is started and idle, the driver accepts
 *   operations and live reconfigurations.
 * - <b>ACTIVE</b>, an asynchronous operation is in progress, used by the
 *   drivers having such operations.
 * - <b>STOPPING</b>, the low level driver is stopping the peripheral.
 * .
 * The callback drivers also have four transient states, used only while
 * the callback is executing to tell its reason:
 * - <b>HALF</b> and <b>FULL</b>, the first or the second half of the buffer
 *   of a circular transfer has been processed.
 * - <b>COMPLETE</b>, the operation has completed.
 * - <b>ERROR</b>, the operation has failed.
 * .
 * When the callback returns the driver goes to the state that follows the
 * event, for example READY at the end of a transfer, unless the callback
 * has changed the state itself, for example by starting a new operation.
 *
 * @section xhal_start_stop Start and Stop
 * drvStart() starts a driver in the STOP state: the low level driver
 * physically initializes the peripheral using the specified configuration,
 * or a default configuration if @p NULL is specified. Every driver can be
 * started without an application-provided configuration. On a successful
 * start the driver goes READY, otherwise it returns to STOP with one of:
 * | Error                      | Meaning                                    |
 * |----------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
 * | @p HAL_RET_CONFIG_ERROR    | The configuration has been rejected.       |
 * | @p HAL_RET_NO_RESOURCE     | A required resource, for example a DMA channel, is not available. |
 * | @p HAL_RET_HW_BUSY         | A required hardware resource is in use.    |
 * | @p HAL_RET_HW_FAILURE      | The hardware failed, for example a timeout. |
 *
 * Calling drvStart() on a READY driver with a @p NULL configuration does
 * nothing, with a configuration it performs a live reconfiguration, see
 * @ref xhal_configurations. In the UNINIT, STARTING and STOPPING states, and
 * while an operation is in progress, it returns @p HAL_RET_INV_STATE.<br>
 * drvStop() stops a started driver in any state, an operation in progress
 * is aborted, the low level driver is required to handle it cleanly. It
 * does nothing on a driver that is stopped, starting or stopping.<br>
 * Starting and stopping a driver from different threads at the same time
 * is not serialized, a thread can get @p HAL_RET_INV_STATE. Threads sharing
 * a driver should lock it around these operations, see @ref xhal_sharing.
 * @note    On SMP systems the callbacks are executed by the core serving the
 *          driver interrupts. When drvStop() is called from another core it
 *          can return while the last callback is still executing, the
 *          resources used by the callback must not be released until it has
 *          returned. Some low level drivers require drvStop() to be called
 *          from the core that started the driver.
 *
 * @section xhal_configurations Configurations
 * A configuration is a structure of type @p hal_xxx_config_t, its content
 * is mostly platform-dependent. The driver keeps a pointer to the
 * configuration, the structure is not copied and must stay valid while in
 * use.<br>
 * A READY driver can be reconfigured without stopping it:
 * - drvSetCfgX() applies a configuration structure.
 * - drvSelectCfgX() applies one of the predefined configurations, selected
 *   by index.
 * .
 * Both functions are X-class and do not take any lock, the caller must make
 * sure that no operation is started on the driver meanwhile.<br>
 * Configuration zero always exists. By default it is the only predefined
 * configuration and it is the default configuration of the driver. Drivers
 * with an @p XXX_USE_CONFIGURATIONS setting can have more: when the setting
 * is enabled the application defines a table named
 * @p xxx_configurations, of type @p xxx_configurations_t, and the index
 * selects one of its entries.<br>
 * Selecting configurations by index keeps the hardware details out of the
 * application code. The XHAL VIO platform, which runs XHAL applications in
 * a sandbox, only accepts configurations selected by index, the
 * configuration structures are held by the host.
 *
 * @section xhal_callbacks Callbacks
 * A callback driver has a single callback, of type @p drv_cb_t, receiving
 * the driver object as parameter:
 * - The callback is set with drvSetCallbackX() and read with
 *   drvGetCallbackX(). It can be changed at any time, @p NULL disables it.
 * - The callback is invoked from the driver interrupt handler, outside the
 *   system lock: I-class functions must be called within
 *   chSysLockFromISR() and chSysUnlockFromISR().
 * - The reason of the invocation is the transient state, checked with
 *   drvStateIsHalfX(), drvStateIsCompleteX() and drvStateIsErrorX(). Some
 *   drivers have additional event information, for example the SIO events.
 * - The driver argument, set with drvSetArgumentX() and read with
 *   drvGetArgumentX(), is a pointer for the use of the callback. The
 *   complex drivers use it to find themselves from the callback of the
 *   driver they own.
 * .
 *
 * @section xhal_operations Operations
 * Drivers with asynchronous operations offer functions starting an
 * operation and returning immediately, for example spiStartExchange(). The
 * driver goes ACTIVE and the callback is invoked at the end of the
 * operation. The I-class variants, for example spiStartExchangeI(), can be
 * called from a callback to chain operations.<br>
 * When the @p XXX_USE_SYNCHRONIZATION setting of a driver is enabled, the
 * driver also offers:
 * - Synchronous functions, for example spiExchange(), which start an
 *   operation and wait for its end.
 * - Synchronization functions, for example spiSynchronizeState() or
 *   sioSynchronizeRX(), which wait for an event with a timeout.
 * .
 * A waiting thread is released with @p MSG_RESET if the operation is
 * stopped or fails.<br>
 * Driver functions return the codes of type @p msg_t: @p HAL_RET_SUCCESS
 * or one of the errors above, plus @p HAL_RET_UNKNOWN_CTL, an unknown
 * control code, @p HAL_RET_IS_INVALID, an invalid instance, and
 * @p HAL_RET_INV_STATE, a request in an invalid driver state. The codes
 * are the same in the classic HAL and are also transferred across the
 * sandbox virtual I/O. Wrong parameters and preconditions, for example
 * buffer sizes and alignments, are checked by debug assertions.
 *
 * @section xhal_sharing Drivers Sharing and Registry
 * The driver functions do not serialize the threads using the same driver.
 * When @p HAL_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION is enabled, each driver has a mutex,
 * taken with drvLock() and released with drvUnlock(), so threads sharing a
 * driver, for example a SPI bus, can perform sequences of operations
 * without interference. The mutex is a kernel mutex, or a binary semaphore
 * if mutexes are disabled in the kernel.<br>
 * When @p HAL_USE_REGISTRY is enabled, each driver object is linked into a
 * registry at initialization and removed when disposed. The drivers are
 * enumerated with drvRegGetFirstX() and drvRegGetNextX(). Each driver has a
 * name, @p "unk" by default, set with drvSetNameX() and read with
 * drvGetNameX().
 *
 * @section xhal_platforms Platforms
 * The source files of a driver are organized in layers:
 * - The portable part, in @p os/xhal/include and @p os/xhal/src, is
 *   generated from the XML descriptions in @p os/xhal/codegen by the code
 *   generator in @p tools/ftl. Generated files must not be edited, the XML
 *   descriptions are modified and the files regenerated.
 * - The low level drivers, in @p os/xhal/ports, are shared among the
 *   platforms by peripheral version, for example
 *   @p ports/STM32/LLD/USARTv3.
 * - A platform, for example @p ports/STM32/STM32G4xx, selects the low level
 *   drivers of a family of devices and provides the platform-wide code,
 *   for example clocks, interrupts and DMA. The @p templates directory
 *   contains templates of low level drivers and the @p stub platform is a
 *   minimal platform without drivers.
 * - The board files are shared with the classic HAL, in @p os/hal/boards.
 * .
 * The VIO platform, in @p ports/vio, implements the drivers on top of the
 * sandbox virtual I/O, so XHAL applications can run in a sandbox.<br>
 * The frequency of the clock points of the platform is returned by
 * halClockGetPointX(). Platforms with clock management can switch clock
 * configuration at runtime with halClockSwitchMode().<br>
 * A project includes @p xhal.mk, which adds the sources of XHAL, only those
 * of the drivers enabled in @p xhalconf.h with the smart build, the
 * @p platform.mk of the platform and the @p board.mk of the board.
 *
 * @section xhal_config Configuration
 * Two files configure XHAL in a project:
 * - @p xhalconf.h, the portable settings: @p HAL_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION,
 *   @p HAL_USE_REGISTRY, the @p HAL_USE_XXX setting enabling each driver
 *   and the settings of each driver, for example @p XXX_USE_SYNCHRONIZATION
 *   and @p XXX_USE_CONFIGURATIONS.
 * - @p xmcuconf.h, the device settings: the driver objects to be created,
 *   the clocks, the interrupt priorities and the DMA assignments.
 * .
 * Both files are updated to the current settings by the scripts in
 * @p tools/updater. @p HAL_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION requires
 * @p CH_CFG_USE_MUTEXES or @p CH_CFG_USE_SEMAPHORES in the kernel.
 */
